On Sun, 9 Apr 2006, L wrote:
[I wrote:]
>> Tongue-in-cheek rationale: The ring of teleport interacts with the
>> wearer's metabolic rate, so creatures with faster metabolisms teleport
>> more often.
>
> Except that such behaviour is not evident in cases of polyself. And I'm
> not sure if I want the poor player to try and work out a detail as
> esoteric as that.

   Sorry, I wasn't suggesting that the "metabolic rate" thing be reflected 
in the implementation, or that the user should need to figure that out.
The faster-metabolism story was supposed to be like the stories I and
others have proposed to explain why you can't enter doors diagonally
(big doorjambs), or move over boulders even though they're easy enough
to push (they're spherical and thus hard to climb), and so on.  I was
just giving a rationale for my proposed "monsters teleport more often"
game mechanic, in case anyone would suggest it went against internal
consistency.

   (And polyself generally doesn't decrease the player's average lifespan
by very much, if at all; therefore, by the mouse-elephant argument, we
may conclude that polyself also doesn't increase the player's metabolic
rate, and therefore doesn't increase his chances of teleporting. :)

